
This directory is intended for PlatformIO Test Runner and project tests.

Unit Testing is a software testing method by which individual units of
source code, sets of one or more MCU program modules together with associated
control data, usage procedures, and operating procedures, are tested to
determine whether they are fit for use. Unit testing finds problems early
in the development cycle.

More information about PlatformIO Unit Testing:
- https://docs.platformio.org/en/latest/advanced/unit-testing/index.html

Native USB MIDI regression checks (Python 3 and g++ or clang++; use WSL):

	python3 test/test_usb_midi_tx.py
	python3 test/test_output_batching.py --check-baseline

These compile the actual project callbacks, MIDI output logic, and transmit
queue with lightweight host-side stubs. They do not replace a firmware build
or hardware timing/stress tests. If the saved checkpoint archive is available,
it supplies negative controls for unsafe ISR transmission and split batching.
The batching test stubs event generation; it does not cover direct process()
calls made by shared-library patterns.

Workshop note echoes now enter a 128-packet SDK queue, initialized in setup.
Producers are the project's core-0 foreground and interrupt callbacks. USB
transmission drains only in unmasked core-0 foreground code, before and after
MIDI reception, with at most 64 packets per drain call. Local gates and samples
remain immediate. USB backpressure retains the unsent packet; SysEx defers the
drain rather than interleaving note packets with a frame.

If the queue overflows, it discards pending echoes, suppresses new echoes until
recovery completes, and sends All Sound Off (CC 120) on all 16 MIDI channels.
This deliberately silences external MIDI instead of silently losing note-offs.
Local playback continues. Inspect usb_midi_tx_queue.overflow_count and
usb_midi_tx_queue.dropped_count in the debugger. This guards Workshop note TX;
it does not prove every other TinyUSB/CDC entry point is interrupt-safe.

The note wrapper no longer prints over CDC from note callbacks or sample
lookup, including invalid sample paths. The native test uses the actual lookup
and note methods with debugging enabled and a connected console to catch
accidental interrupt-side serial writes. Parameter streaming stays foreground.

The fork's task fix and RAM tracing are separate. Tracing defaults off; the
rpipico_16mb_usb_trace environment enables it explicitly. Run the recorder and
task-routing checks from the project directory with:

	python3 lib/Adafruit_TinyUSB_Arduino/tools/test_pico_trace.py

Hardware follow-up: repeat the MIDI/CDC stress in rpipico_16mb without tracing,
check repeated Start/Stop and note-offs, reconnect USB, and test an open console
whose host stops reading. Monitor the queue overflow/drop counters under load.
RP2350 is not yet validated. The extreme-tempo reacquisition glitch is separate
from the captured USB lockups and has not been diagnosed here.
